==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} ''[[internecivus|internecīvus]]'', '''[[deadly]]''', alternatively, Latin ''[[inter]]'' ([[between]]) and ''[[necare]]'' (to slay).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˌɪntəˈniːsaɪn/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˌɪntɚˈnɛsin/}}
* {{audio|en-us-internecine.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# [[mutually|Mutually]] [[destructive]]; most often applied to [[warfare]].
#: '' '''Internecine''' strife in Gaza claimed its most senior victim yesterday when militants assassinated one of the most hated security chiefs there.''
# Characterized by [[struggle]] within a group, usually applied to an [[ethnic]] or [[familial]] relationship.
#: ''The [[w:Mongol|Mongol]] people were plagued by '''internecine''' conflict until [[w:Genghis Khan|Genghis Khan]] unified them and focused their aggression outwards on other peoples.''
#* {{quote-book|year={{circa2|1900}}|author=[[w:Mark Twain|Mark Twain]]|title=Autobiography of Mark Twain|year_published=2010|passage=During the year of my engagement — 1869 — while I was out on the lecture platform, the daily letter that came for me generally brought me news from the front — by which expression I refer to the '''internecine''' war that was always going on in a friendly way between these two orthographists about the spelling of words.}}

